---
import Root from '#/layout/root.astro'
import { Image } from 'astro:assets'
import vibeCheck from '#/assets/vibe-check.png'
import vibeCheckModeration from '#/assets/vibe-check-moderation.png'
import vibeCheckGame from '#/assets/vibe-check-gameview.png'
import vibeCheckTarget from '#/assets/vibe-check-target.png'
---

<Root title="Vibe Check!">
  <article class="prose flow">
    <h1>Case Study: Vibe Check!</h1>
    <p class="text-lg">
      <a href="https://vibecheck.mxbranch.com" target="_blank">Vibe Check!</a> is
      a web-based multiplayer game designed for Twitch streamers to play with their
      chat, made in conjunction with streamer <a
        href="https://jackdaw.tv"
        target="_blank"
      >
        Jackdaw.tv
      </a>.
    </p>
    <figure class="prose__fullwidth flow">
      <Image src={vibeCheck} alt="Vibe Check! Welcome Screen" />
      <figcaption>The initial welcome screen of the game.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <section class="prose__fullwidth with-sidebar">
      <div class="sidebar">
        <h2>Requirements</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="flow">
        <p>
          Jack was interested in creating a version of the game <a
            href="https://www.cmyk.games/collections/games/products/wavelength"
            target="_blank"
          >
            Wavelength
          </a> by CMYK Games which they could play with their Twitch stream audience.
          It needed:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Stream safety features such as moderation</li>
          <li>To be accessible from mobile devices</li>
          <li>To have a polished user interface</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="with-sidebar prose__fullwidth">
      <div class="sidebar">
        <h2>Tools and Libraries</h2>
      </div>
      <div>
        <ul role="list">
          <li>React</li>
          <li>Tanstack Start / Query / Form</li>
          <li>Drizzle + Postgres</li>
          <li>Better-Auth</li>
          <li>Tailwind</li>
          <li>Valibot</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>
    <h2>Choosing Libraries</h2>
    <p>
      This game required UI that responds in real time to the game state, so I
      elected to make a React <abbr title="Single Page Application">SPA</abbr> with
      an API for server-side data fetching and mutations. I chose Tanstack Start as
      a framework since it provides everything I needed: routing capabilities, being
      able to define type-safe <abbr title="Remote Procedure Calls">RPCs</abbr> for
      data manipulation on the server, and compatibility with the other libraries
      I'd chosen for the project. It was also an excuse to give it a go, which is
      something I'd been trying to find for a little while!
    </p>

    <p>
      Other libraries were chosen largely through pragmatism: this was a fairly
      quick turnaround so I wanted to use libraries I was familiar with and knew
      could get the job done. Tanstack Query would let me control repeating data
      queries based on game state, meaning I didn't need to write any complex
      re-fetching timer logic. Drizzle gave me database schema management as
      well as flexible query design. Valibot let me share validation logic
      between the client and server, and being <a
        href="https://standardschema.dev/"
        target="_blank"
      >
        Standard Schema
      </a> compatible means it integrates with Tanstack Form nicely. Tailwind was
      a choice made for some initial rapid prototyping which ended up sticking around
      into the final project due to the aforementioned quick turnaround.
    </p>

    <h2>The User Interface</h2>

    <p>
      This project needed a design meeting two distinct requirements:
      accessibility on a wide variety of devices, and being a fun thing to look
      at for at least an hour of streaming time! Once I had the business logic
      of the game sorted, I spent time making mobile-first user interfaces for
      each route. I tried to avoid using breakpoints and media queries for this
      where possible, instead leaning on flexbox and grid to do most of the
      work. Where media queries were used, they were for adjusting text size,
      padding, or occasionally flex direction.
    </p>

    <p>
      Once I had the overall layout for each route sorted, I then added a number
      of small design features and interactions with the aim of giving the game
      its own personality. This included design features like adding a crown to
      users who had permission to run games, adding a radial 'spotlight' effect
      to the background which slowly rotates, and making the buttons respond
      with a little motion when hovered over.
    </p>

    <figure class="prose__fullwidth flow" style="--figure-img-y: 61%;">
      <!-- Don't pay attention to the magic number behind the curtain -->
      <Image src={vibeCheckGame} alt="Vibe Check! Game View" />
      <figcaption>
        The main interface for the streamer to guess based on user prompts. The
        slider interface is prominent on the screen and shows the streamer's
        avatar as the handle. The chat player's prompt is shown as a speech
        bubble coming from their avatar, giving players a little moment in the
        spotlight.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure class="prose__fullwidth flow" style="--figure-img-y: 59%;">
      <!-- Don't pay attention to the magic number behind the curtain -->
      <Image
        src={vibeCheckTarget}
        alt="Vibe Check! Game View showing the prompt target"
      />
      <figcaption>
        Once a guess is locked in, the target is shown as a series of concentric
        circles, giving immediate visual feedback about the accuracy of the
        streamer's guess. As you can see here, I guessed... poorly. <!-- The vibes were rancid. -->
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>Stream Safety</h2>

    <p>
      Twitch is a platform with fairly rigid <abbr title="Terms of Service">
        ToS
      </abbr>, and any public stream is a potential target for trolls. Any time
      you're getting user input there's risks you need to account for, and in
      this case one of the risks is "anything a malicious individual types in
      might then be displayed to several dozen people and risk the streamer
      punishment from the platform". Tooling to minimize risk and ensure stream
      safety was paramount for this project. To this end there are multiple
      systems in place:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        A soft-gate of requiring the user to sign in with an active Twitch
        account. This doesn't fully reduce the risk of trolling, but it
        introduces friction to participation and reduces the likelihood a bot or
        rogue agent will join a game. This also let me implement a feature to
        let the streamer block accounts making unsavoury prompts.
      </li>
      <li>
        A 'moderation preview window' which could be kept on a second monitor.
        This window shows the <em>next prompt</em> that will be shown, along with
        options to skip that prompt or block the user. Using this allows the streamer
        to vet content before it is shown to stream, and enact moderation quickly.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <figure class="prose__fullwidth flow" style="--figure-img-y: 53%;">
      <!-- Don't pay attention to the magic number behind the curtain -->
      <Image src={vibeCheckModeration} alt="Vibe Check! Moderation View" />
      <figcaption>
        The moderation view allows the streamer to check the next prompt and
        engage in moderation actions.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p></p>
  </article>
</Root>
